d342b9cf65f93fe58e77aa8dcd99a7cde0880f68378862fa.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace since in does not contain the zero-vector.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "619d88ad-a1c7-5b3d-8c24-b6a78974ec7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459847} {:answer/id #uuid "543df3d1-82cc-50ab-9e90-e99e3210da09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "619d88ad-a1c7-5b3d-8c24-b6a78974ec7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459847} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf642b5-0d99-5a3e-8aa9-2b628925bcd1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18b93bd-bcdd-50e4-afa3-95fee527dc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459849} {:answer/id #uuid "41400ea3-a328-570f-81df-c3348d63b6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41400ea3-a328-570f-81df-c3348d63b6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459850} :answer-field/id #uuid "56504409-bf23-53eb-a559-4baad15a7cde" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460791} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192fb319-480e-5a3f-bc8b-a5bec8b0c334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459851} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e8fcbf-9d24-5fd5-ae32-89e8ef38aa0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192fb319-480e-5a3f-bc8b-a5bec8b0c334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459851} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca5cf1d-3106-5e01-b467-cfe14762ca39" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d3ff8e-37f2-5365-be21-7d8861823943" :question/math-academy-id "q-35595" :question/problem "![](images/df/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c29d621-f793-5227-8c45-cdc973193a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459853} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e0b353-6c44-5848-84f3-ca21bd8a12f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c29d621-f793-5227-8c45-cdc973193a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459853} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c63b21-f398-5ff0-8619-174f250b0c41" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313ba5ba-4b76-5ce8-89d7-ca607ead8684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459855} {:answer/id #uuid "50f64642-889e-55ea-970c-b9b08f2e4f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "313ba5ba-4b76-5ce8-89d7-ca607ead8684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459855} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5befb55-bf65-517b-b35a-8ddfd6d9c0c0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac6fd3d-18ec-57f8-aeb6-c69b06f785dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459857} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d506b5-c66a-532f-bcab-23809b19c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d506b5-c66a-532f-bcab-23809b19c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459858} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6fc26cd-9cd3-5000-bc44-9e01a45d4536" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff2c4e7d-b661-5abc-a5ef-3c554262f85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35862" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<4\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1\\le {x}^{2}\\le 9,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1<y<4\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79b038c-fe01-5ebc-866e-1019c3411829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460161} {:answer/id #uuid "7e520372-4f2a-52fe-80ef-779b4c47f5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e520372-4f2a-52fe-80ef-779b4c47f5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460162} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f28ff6b-8064-54e6-ac2a-cbfc20687b92" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc8c9ed-7eb1-530d-8dc2-74b72a9c7683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460163} {:answer/id #uuid "c32fe42f-7550-5e6f-89b8-a8cf262268b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebc8c9ed-7eb1-530d-8dc2-74b72a9c7683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460163} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c4ff18-793c-5bf5-b61b-dc2775e56694" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0832d56b-30c1-5f4f-a9de-e199bf8d8ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460165} {:answer/id #uuid "f481190f-e908-5178-98b4-a892e227e637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0832d56b-30c1-5f4f-a9de-e199bf8d8ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460165} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1215464-5386-54ed-a118-8ea6861f3e56" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a3b9409-39a3-5316-8079-d83249ab7774" :question/math-academy-id "q-71808" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n- **I.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0<y<{x}^{2},\\,0<x<1}$\n- **II.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le {y}^{2}\\le 4}$\n- **III.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b4c5e4-e959-56e2-a295-57ff1957f4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459587} {:answer/id #uuid "35bc7942-5cb9-5f6e-a604-01bbc56e91d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35bc7942-5cb9-5f6e-a604-01bbc56e91d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459588} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d30ed0-77d5-5679-93b2-0ea48f44edef" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6680343a-55ac-5674-af4a-41f728522fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459589} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea09bb3-fab5-5fa4-9de9-6cc3c6ceedb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6680343a-55ac-5674-af4a-41f728522fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459589} :answer-field/id #uuid "a56b6579-beea-5a72-b0d9-20005a09ab04" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460661} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a4ac9d-8c4a-5b91-b0b8-c3f46a3d66a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459591} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3da9de-0754-5c44-b6f9-c2f9e21352c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88a4ac9d-8c4a-5b91-b0b8-c3f46a3d66a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459591} :answer-field/id #uuid "323a6fc7-1616-52bd-a614-a99166c8c0e4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9eb2357b-f9d2-552f-8ddb-db8ea02b5422" :question/math-academy-id "q-160481" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb8f3b41-f753-50c9-bbcc-8ee014baf22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459623} {:answer/id #uuid "63945080-8553-53a8-8917-c9ef2adb87bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb8f3b41-f753-50c9-bbcc-8ee014baf22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459623} :answer-field/id #uuid "43dea65b-7921-5c81-8166-bd24d95730e9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a796e3-52fd-59b9-b263-1496fc730b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459625} {:answer/id #uuid "3cedd932-157a-5436-898a-674c4a2cb077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cedd932-157a-5436-898a-674c4a2cb077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459626} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4099f7-b667-5d8f-9e68-0c94bab6cced" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc173c6-feb6-5c9f-ba80-153873d0800a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459627} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81fde8-4689-53c6-b96b-75b580b59783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d81fde8-4689-53c6-b96b-75b580b59783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459628} :answer-field/id #uuid "92b33259-0e10-51b0-9e0c-da44686b7a25" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3f1ffb3-6372-5ba4-9ec3-35379749f5f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-171781" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x\\,|\\,8.$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $x$ is even.\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$\n\nI. If $x\\,|\\,8$ then $x$ is even, or if $x$ is even then $x\\,|\\,8.$\n\nII. If $x\\,|\\,8$ then $x$ is even, and if $x$ is even then $x\\,|\\,8.$\n\nIII. $x\\,|\\,8$ if and only if $x$ is even.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\,|\\,8$ if and only if $x$ is even.\n\n- $x\\,|\\,8$ is equivalent to $x$ is even.\n\n- If $x\\,|\\,8$ then $x$ is even, and if $x$ is even then $x\\,|\\,8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "704e020e-d34a-528d-9e9d-8b82289223c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459647} {:answer/id #uuid "f766b986-cfca-589d-b307-c3cfed4a40df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f766b986-cfca-589d-b307-c3cfed4a40df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459648} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8358696-c4dc-5792-a56d-b4cf06cd1f4e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f32f7ace-9473-5adf-87d1-3a0754c1f4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459649} {:answer/id #uuid "5764e144-7231-5741-8902-987088781ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5764e144-7231-5741-8902-987088781ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459650} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c262398-01aa-5334-93fb-2d94d63b0e3f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0937afd-eb79-5420-8b9b-1cc18cc13046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459651} {:answer/id #uuid "971d785f-c08d-59dc-8767-b018db2c3a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0937afd-eb79-5420-8b9b-1cc18cc13046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459651} :answer-field/id #uuid "45cf6747-f781-581e-9df2-b7717fa5597b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3a9664a-054f-5dcf-9b0e-e1b4ab3dd171" :question/math-academy-id "q-174456" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $x<y$\n\nII. $n$ is a perfect square.\n\nIII. Are $n$ and $m$ coprime?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(n,m)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218